  "content": {
    "id": "p51-q5-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "5",
    "prompt": "Show that if $x+1$ is a factor of the polynomial $x^n+1$, then $n$ must be odd.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$n$ must be odd, since the factor theorem gives $(-1)^n=-1$.",
      "worked": "By the factor theorem,\n$$\\begin{align*}(-1)^n+1&=0\\\\(-1)^n&=-1.\\end{align*}$$\nAn integer power of $-1$ is $-1$ only when the exponent is odd. Hence $n$ must be odd.",
